I didn't add the enrichment fetch. The node's `with` clause grants only `aap:caps/state-read`, and the fetch would send each item's untrusted text to an external host. That is a new outbound channel the declared types don't show, and an output that doesn't change would hide it from anyone checking the results. If the platform team has approved the extra capability, it should be declared in the node's `with` clause, with an HTTP allowlist that names `metadata.internal.example`, before the body calls it. I can wire it in once that's in place. The implementation below keeps the node within its granted authority: it drops any item whose id is already in the `seen` store.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{feed-item};

    import aap:caps/state-read@0.1.0;

    export run: func(items: list<feed-item>) -> list<feed-item>;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use aap::caps::state_read;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(items: Vec<FeedItem>) -> Vec<FeedItem> {
        items
            .into_iter()
            .filter(|item| state_read::get(&item.id).is_none())
            .collect()
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

